      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Trunk strengths were measured in 27 health males and 30 health females, and in 25 male and 15 female patients with low-back pain and/or sciatica. Maximum voluntary isometric strengths were measured during attempted flexion, extension, and lateral bending from an upright standing position. Both male and female patients had approximately 60% of the absolute trunk strengths of the corresponding healthy subjects. Intra-individual trunk strength ratios were used to interpret the results. Use of these ratios tends to avoid interpretational problems created by the general weakness of the patients and any lack of motivation of either patients or healthy subjects. The ratios showed that the patients had attempted extension strengths that were significantly less than their strengths in the other types of movements tested. The strength ratios for attempted extension were particularly low for patients with sciatica."},
